/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/ccsp.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("ccsp", [["cp1",0,1,"A department says its new 'private cloud' requires a ticket and two weeks of manual work by the infrastructure team for every new virtual machine. Which NIST essential characteristic is MOST clearly missing?",["Broad network access","Resource pooling","Measured service","On-demand self-service"],3,"On-demand self-service means consumers provision resources themselves without human interaction with the provider. A manual ticket process fails it; the other characteristics may still be present.","Domain 1",["Broad network access is about reaching services over standard networks from many devices, which the scenario does not contradict.","Resource pooling concerns serving many consumers from shared resources; a slow approval process does not show pooling is absent.","Measured service is metering and reporting usage, which can exist even when provisioning is manual.",null],null],["cp2",0,1,"In which cloud service model is the customer responsible for patching the guest operating system?",["Platform as a Service (PaaS)","Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)","Software as a Service (SaaS)","Function as a Service (serverless)"],1,"In IaaS the customer manages everything from the guest operating system up. In PaaS, SaaS and serverless the provider manages the operating system.","Domain 1",["In PaaS the provider runs and patches the operating system and runtime; the customer manages its code and data.",null,"In SaaS the provider operates the whole application stack, including the operating system.","Serverless functions run on provider-managed infrastructure, so the customer never patches an operating system."],null],["cp3",0,1,"An employee shares a SaaS folder containing customer records with 'anyone with the link', and the records leak. Under the shared responsibility model, who is responsible for this failure?",["The customer, because sharing settings and data access are its responsibility","The SaaS provider, because it operates the application","The SaaS provider and customer equally, because SaaS responsibilities are shared evenly","The identity provider, because it authenticated the employee"],0,"In every service model the customer controls its data, users and configuration choices such as sharing. The provider's platform worked as designed.","Domain 1",[null,"Operating the application does not make the provider responsible for how the customer configures sharing of its own data.","SaaS responsibilities are not split evenly; data, access and configuration decisions sit with the customer.","The identity provider authenticated a legitimate user correctly; the leak came from a sharing decision, not an authentication failure."],null],["cp4",0,1,"Several regional hospitals with identical regulatory obligations want to share the cost of a cloud environment that only they can use. Which deployment model fits BEST?",["Public cloud","Hybrid cloud","Community cloud","Multi-cloud"],2,"A community cloud serves a group of organizations with shared concerns, such as the same regulations, and spreads governance and cost among them.","Domain 1",["A public cloud is open to any customer, which does not meet the goal of restricting use to the hospitals.","Hybrid cloud describes linking distinct clouds such as private and public, not sharing one environment among peers.",null,"Multi-cloud means using more than one provider; it says nothing about restricting use to a group of organizations."],null],["cp5",0,1,"Which party acts on a customer's behalf to negotiate, aggregate or integrate services from several cloud providers?",["Cloud service broker","Cloud auditor","Cloud service provider","Regulator"],0,"A cloud service broker is a partner that intermediates between customers and providers, negotiating and combining services.","Domain 1",[null,"A cloud auditor independently assesses controls; it does not negotiate or combine services for the customer.","The provider offers its own services; it does not act for the customer across other providers.","A regulator sets and enforces rules; it does not procure or integrate services."],null],["cp6",0,1,"A company is leaving a public cloud storage service and cannot supervise how the provider wipes its disks. What is the MOST reliable way to make its remaining data unrecoverable?",["Ask the provider to degauss the drives that held the data","Destroy the customer-controlled keys that encrypted the data","Overwrite each object once with zeros before deleting the account","Delete the objects and rely on the provider's standard deletion"],1,"Crypto-shredding works in shared infrastructure: if data was encrypted with keys only the customer controls, destroying all copies of the keys leaves every replica unreadable.","Domain 1",["Providers do not degauss specific drives for one tenant in shared storage, and degaussing does not work on SSDs.",null,"Overwriting through the service API cannot be verified across replicas and underlying media the customer never sees.","Standard deletion may leave recoverable remnants and replicas, so it gives the least assurance."],null],["cp7",0,1,"Why do containers generally provide weaker isolation than virtual machines?",["Containers cannot use network segmentation","Containers always run with root privileges on the host","Containers share the host operating system kernel","Containers store data only in ephemeral volumes"],2,"All containers on a host share its kernel, so a kernel vulnerability or privileged container can affect other containers; VMs each run their own kernel on a hypervisor.","Domain 1",["Containers can be segmented with network policies, so this is not the reason for weaker isolation.","Running as root is a common misconfiguration, not a requirement; containers can and should run as non-root users.",null,"Containers can use persistent volumes; storage type is unrelated to kernel-level isolation."],null],["cp8",0,1,"A partner bank will share card data for fraud analysis only if the cloud provider's administrators cannot read it even while it is being processed. Which technology addresses this requirement?",["Server-side encryption of all storage volumes and database snapshots","TLS between the partner bank and the application","Tokenization of the card data at rest","Confidential computing with a trusted execution environment"],3,"Confidential computing protects data in use by processing it inside a hardware enclave that the host OS and hypervisor cannot read.","Domain 1",["Storage encryption protects data at rest; the data is decrypted in memory during processing.","TLS protects data in transit only, not while the application processes it.","Tokenization replaces values at rest, but analysis of real card data would still expose it in memory.",null],null],["cp9",0,1,"A business impact analysis states that the order system may lose no more than 15 minutes of data. Which metric has been defined?",["Recovery time objective (RTO)","Recovery point objective (RPO)","Maximum tolerable downtime (MTD)","Mean time to repair (MTTR)"],1,"RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time before the disruption, which drives backup and replication frequency.","Domain 1",["RTO is how quickly the service must be restored, not how much data may be lost.",null,"MTD is the longest the business can tolerate an outage, which concerns downtime rather than data loss.","MTTR is an operational average for repairing components, not a business recovery target."],null],["cp10",0,1,"A customer wants the strongest independent evidence that a provider's security controls actually worked throughout the past year. Which report should it request?",["SOC 2 Type II","SOC 2 Type I","SOC 3","CSA STAR Level 1 self-assessment"],0,"A SOC 2 Type II report tests the operating effectiveness of controls over a period, typically six to twelve months.","Domain 1",[null,"A Type I report evaluates control design at a single point in time, not operation over a year.","SOC 3 is a general-use summary without the detailed test results needed for due diligence.","A STAR Level 1 entry is a self-assessment, so it is not independent evidence."],null],["cp11",0,1,"Which standard provides a code of practice specifically for protecting personally identifiable information in public clouds acting as processors?",["ISO/IEC 27017","ISO/IEC 27001","ISO/IEC 27018","ISO/IEC 15408"],2,"ISO/IEC 27018 focuses on PII protection by public cloud processors. ISO/IEC 27017 covers cloud security controls more generally.","Domain 1",["ISO/IEC 27017 gives cloud-specific security controls but is not focused on PII protection.","ISO/IEC 27001 specifies requirements for an ISMS, not cloud PII practices.",null,"ISO/IEC 15408 is Common Criteria, used to evaluate products."],null],["cp12",0,1,"A regulation requires that encryption protecting certain government data use cryptographic modules validated to the current U.S. standard. Which validation should the security team look for?",["Common Criteria EAL4","ISO/IEC 27017 certification","CSA STAR attestation","FIPS 140-3"],3,"FIPS 140-3 is the current U.S. standard for validating cryptographic modules, replacing FIPS 140-2.","Domain 1",["Common Criteria evaluates product assurance against a protection profile; it is not the cryptographic module validation named here.","ISO/IEC 27017 certifies cloud security controls in a management system, not individual cryptographic modules.","CSA STAR attestation covers a provider's controls against the CCM, not module validation.",null],null],["cp13",0,1,"Before approving a hosted large language model service for summarizing confidential contracts, what should the security team verify FIRST in the provider's terms?",["Whether the model scores well on public accuracy and reasoning benchmarks for legal text","Whether prompts and data are retained or used to train the provider's models","Whether the service supports the most languages","Whether the provider offers the lowest price per request"],1,"Confidential data sent in prompts may be stored or used for training; data use, retention and location terms are the key security and legal questions.","Domain 1",["Benchmark accuracy is a quality question and says nothing about how confidential data is handled.",null,"Language support is a functional requirement, not a data protection control.","Price is a procurement factor and does not address the confidentiality risk."],null],["cp14",0,1,"An attacker sends a support chatbot a message containing hidden instructions that make it reveal its system prompt and internal tool list. What type of attack is this?",["Data poisoning","Model inversion","Prompt injection","Membership inference"],2,"Prompt injection uses crafted input to override an AI model's instructions at runtime.","Domain 1",["Data poisoning corrupts training data before or during training, not a live conversation.","Model inversion tries to reconstruct training data from model outputs, not to override instructions.",null,"Membership inference tests whether a specific record was in the training set."],null],["cp15",0,1,"What is the main security drawback of adopting a multi-cloud strategy?",["More complexity in keeping identity, logging and policy consistent","Greater dependence on a single provider's proprietary services","Loss of the ability to encrypt data at rest","Inability to use infrastructure as code"],0,"Each provider has different identity, logging and security tooling, so consistent governance and skills are harder. Multi-cloud reduces, rather than increases, single-provider lock-in.","Domain 1",[null,"Multi-cloud reduces dependence on one provider; greater lock-in is the opposite of its effect.","All major providers support encryption at rest, so multi-cloud does not remove it.","Infrastructure as code tools can target multiple providers."],null],["cp16",0,2,"In the cloud data lifecycle, when should data FIRST be classified?",["Store","Use","Share","Create"],3,"Classification should be applied at creation (including modification) so every later phase can use the label to choose controls.","Domain 2",["Store follows create; waiting until storage leaves data briefly unlabeled and uncontrolled.","Use is too late; data may already be stored and shared without the right controls.","Share is later still; unclassified data could be shared inappropriately before anyone labels it.",null],null],["cp17",0,2,"An online store wants its order, analytics and support systems to stop handling real card numbers so they fall out of PCI DSS scope, while payments still work. Which technique BEST fits?",["Hashing each card number with SHA-256","Tokenization through a secured token vault","Static masking of the card numbers","Encrypting the card database with provider-managed keys"],1,"Tokens have no mathematical relationship to card numbers, and only the vault can map them back, so systems handling tokens can be removed from much of the PCI scope.","Domain 2",["Card numbers have limited possible values, so unsalted hashes can be brute-forced and hashing cannot be reversed for payments.",null,"Static masking permanently alters the data, so payments needing the real number would not work.","Encrypted card data is still cardholder data, and systems that can decrypt it remain in scope."],null],["cp18",0,2,"A development team needs a copy of production customer data for testing, without exposing real personal details to testers. Which approach is MOST appropriate?",["Dynamic data masking on the production database","Encrypting the test database with the production key","Static data masking of the test copy","Granting testers read-only access to production"],2,"Static masking creates a permanently masked copy that keeps realistic formats for testing while removing real values.","Domain 2",["Dynamic masking hides values at query time in production; testers would still work against production data.","Encryption protects data at rest, but testers need to read the data, so it would be decrypted for them.",null,"Read-only access to production still exposes real personal data to testers."],null],["cp19",0,2,"Which statement correctly distinguishes pseudonymized data from anonymized data?",["Pseudonymized data can be re-identified using separately held information","Anonymized data can be re-identified with the original key","Pseudonymized data falls outside privacy laws such as the GDPR because names are removed","Anonymized data must always be encrypted at rest"],0,"Pseudonymization replaces identifiers with codes that can be linked back; it remains personal data. Truly anonymized data cannot be linked back to a person.","Domain 2",[null,"Anonymized data, by definition, cannot be re-identified; a key that restores identity means it is only pseudonymized.","Pseudonymized data is still personal data under the GDPR because it can be re-identified.","Anonymized data is no longer personal data, so no encryption requirement follows from privacy law."],null],["cp20",0,2,"A regulator requires that a bank's cloud provider must never be able to decrypt certain records without the bank's live participation. Which key management approach meets this?",["Bring your own key imported into the provider's KMS","Provider-managed keys with automatic rotation","Customer-managed keys stored in the provider's KMS","Hold your own key (external key management)"],3,"HYOK keeps keys in the customer's own key service outside the provider, so the provider cannot decrypt without calling the customer's system.","Domain 2",["With BYOK the key is imported into the provider's KMS, so the provider's service can use it without the bank's participation.","Provider-managed keys are fully controlled by the provider, the opposite of the requirement.","Customer-managed keys still reside in the provider's KMS, where the provider's service can use them.",null],null],["cp21",0,2,"A code scan finds a database password in an application's configuration file committed to source control. What is the BEST long-term fix?",["Delete the file from the latest commit and keep using the same password afterwards","Base64-encode the password in the configuration file","Rotate the password and have the app fetch it at runtime from a secrets manager","Restrict the repository so only senior developers can read it"],2,"The exposed secret must be rotated, and retrieving it at runtime from a secrets manager (or using a workload identity) removes it from code entirely.","Domain 2",["The password remains in the repository history and is already exposed, so it must be rotated.","Base64 is an encoding, not encryption, and anyone can decode it.",null,"Limiting readers reduces exposure but still stores a live secret in source control."],null],["cp22",0,2,"Which storage type is lost when a cloud instance is stopped or terminated?",["Object storage","Ephemeral storage","Volume (block) storage","Long-term archival storage"],1,"Ephemeral storage is tied to the instance's life and disappears when the instance stops or terminates.","Domain 2",["Object storage is independent of any instance and persists until deleted.",null,"Block volumes are persistent and can be detached and reattached to other instances.","Archival storage is designed for durable long-term retention."],null],["cp23",0,2,"A weekly scan finds that one object storage bucket allows anonymous public reads. After removing the grant, what should the team do to prevent recurrence across the account?",["Rename the bucket so it is harder to guess","Move the bucket to a different region","Enable versioning on the bucket","Enable the account-level block on public access"],3,"An account-wide public access block stops any bucket from being made public by mistake, which addresses the root cause.","Domain 2",["Obscure names are not access control; bucket names are often discoverable.","Region does not affect whether a bucket policy allows public access.","Versioning protects against deletion and overwrites, not against public exposure.",null],null],["cp24",0,2,"Before deploying a cloud DLP solution, what should an organization do FIRST?",["Discover and classify its sensitive data","Enable blocking mode on all outbound channels","Deploy endpoint agents to all laptops","Enable TLS inspection at the network edge"],0,"DLP can only protect data it can recognize; discovery and classification define what to look for and how to treat it.","Domain 2",[null,"Blocking without classification produces many false positives and disrupts legitimate work.","Endpoint agents need policies based on classification to be useful.","TLS inspection gives visibility of traffic but still needs defined sensitive data to act on."],null],["cp25",0,2,"Remote employees upload files from home laptops directly to an unsanctioned SaaS app. Why might the company's network DLP appliance miss this?",["The traffic never passes through the corporate network","Network DLP appliances are only able to inspect outbound email traffic","SaaS applications do not use HTTPS","Network DLP only scans data at rest"],0,"Network DLP sees only traffic that crosses its inspection point; remote users going straight to the internet bypass it. Endpoint or API-based DLP and a CASB close the gap.","Domain 2",[null,"Network DLP can inspect many protocols, not only email.","SaaS apps almost always use HTTPS; that would make inspection harder, not explain why traffic is unseen.","Network DLP inspects data in motion; scanning data at rest is a different DLP type."],null],["cp26",0,2,"A company emailed a confidential design document to a partner who has since left the project. How can the company stop that person from opening copies already downloaded?",["Delete the document from the company's file share","Revoke the person's rights in the IRM system protecting the document","Enable DLP rules that block outbound email attachments to the partner's domain","Change the file share permissions to owner-only"],1,"IRM protection travels with the file; the client must obtain rights from the policy server, so revoking rights blocks access to existing copies.","Domain 2",["Deleting the original does nothing to copies the partner already has.",null,"Outbound DLP can stop future sending but cannot reach a copy already delivered.","Share permissions control the original location, not files already outside it."],null],["cp27",0,2,"Which is a recognized objective of information rights management (IRM)?",["Faster retrieval from archival storage","Automatic deduplication of documents","Persistent protection that follows the data","Replication of documents across regions"],2,"IRM objectives include persistent protection, dynamic policy control, automatic expiration, continuous auditing and interoperability.","Domain 2",["Retrieval speed is a storage performance property, not an IRM objective.","Deduplication is a storage efficiency feature unrelated to usage rights.",null,"Replication supports availability, not control of how content is used."],null],["cp28",0,2,"A lifecycle rule is about to delete chat records whose retention period has expired, but legal counsel has placed them under a litigation hold. What should happen?",["Delete them because the retention period has ended","Archive them and delete the originals on schedule","Delete them after exporting a summary for counsel","Preserve the records until counsel releases the hold"],3,"A legal hold overrides the normal retention schedule; deleting held data can lead to court sanctions.","Domain 2",["Retention periods do not apply while a hold is in force; deletion would violate the preservation duty.","Deleting originals on schedule can destroy metadata and evidence covered by the hold.","A summary is not a substitute for preserving the original records.",null],null],["cp29",0,2,"What must an archiving policy address to make sure encrypted archives can be read at the end of a ten-year retention period?",["Moving the archives to a new region every year to refresh the storage media","Compressing the archives to reduce storage cost","Retention and protection of the decryption keys for the full period","Storing archives in the same account as production"],2,"Archived encrypted data is useless without its keys, so key retention must match the data retention period.","Domain 2",["Moving regions annually adds risk and cost and does nothing for readability.","Compression saves cost but does not help decrypt the data later.",null,"Co-locating archives with production increases risk and does not address key availability."],null],["cp30",0,2,"An investigation cannot determine which engineer deleted a storage bucket because the team shares one administrator account. Which improvement BEST restores accountability?",["Longer retention of management plane logs","Stronger password for the shared administrator account","More frequent backups of the bucket contents","Unique federated identities for each administrator"],3,"Accountability requires attributing each action to an individual; unique identities make logs show who acted.","Domain 2",["Keeping logs longer does not help if every entry shows the same shared account.","A stronger password protects the account but still cannot distinguish between the people using it.","Backups aid recovery but do not show who performed the deletion.",null],null],["cp31",0,2,"Which data type is typically the HARDEST to discover and classify automatically?",["Structured data in relational tables","Unstructured data such as documents and chat messages","Semi-structured JSON records with nested keys and values","Database schemas and column names"],1,"Unstructured data has no schema or keys to read, so discovery depends on inspecting content.","Domain 2",["Structured data has schemas and column names that make discovery comparatively easy.",null,"JSON has keys and values that tools can parse, making it easier than free text.","Schemas and column names are metadata that directly help discovery."],null],["cp32",0,2,"Who should decide the classification level of a customer database?",["The data owner","The data custodian","The cloud service provider","The database administrator"],0,"The data owner is the business role accountable for the data and decides its classification and access.","Domain 2",[null,"The custodian implements controls on the owner's behalf but does not decide classification.","The provider operates infrastructure and has no authority over the customer's data classification.","The DBA is a custodian role that maintains the database, not the accountable owner."],null],["cp33",0,2,"A fraud model starts approving transactions that contain a specific unusual merchant code. Investigators find crafted records were added to its training data. Which control would MOST directly have prevented this?",["Encrypting the model endpoint's TLS traffic","Restricting write access to training data and validating data provenance","Rate-limiting and authenticating every query sent to the deployed model endpoint","Adding more GPU capacity for retraining"],1,"This is data poisoning with a backdoor trigger; controlling who can modify training data and verifying its origin and integrity addresses it.","Domain 2",["TLS protects prompts in transit but does not stop poisoned records entering training data.",null,"Rate limiting slows model extraction, not the tampering of training data.","More compute does nothing to ensure the integrity of the training set."],null],["cp34",0,3,"A bank's core system cannot be shut down for maintenance windows. What is the LOWEST Uptime Institute tier that allows planned maintenance of any component without shutting down IT equipment?",["Tier I","Tier II","Tier IV","Tier III"],3,"Tier III is concurrently maintainable, with multiple distribution paths. Tier IV adds fault tolerance but is not the lowest tier meeting the need.","Domain 3",["Tier I has single paths and no redundancy, so maintenance requires shutdown.","Tier II adds redundant components but still has a single distribution path that forces shutdowns for some work.","Tier IV also allows it, but it is not the lowest tier and costs more than needed.",null],null],["cp35",0,3,"Why do cloud providers use type 1 hypervisors rather than type 2 hypervisors for multitenant hosts?",["Type 1 lets tenants install their own host operating system","Type 1 does not require any patching","Type 1 runs directly on hardware with a smaller attack surface","Type 1 removes the need for tenant isolation"],2,"A bare-metal hypervisor avoids a general-purpose host OS, reducing attack surface and improving performance.","Domain 3",["Tenants never install the host OS; type 1 designs have no general-purpose host OS at all.","All hypervisors need patching; minimal code reduces but does not eliminate flaws.",null,"Isolation is the core purpose of the hypervisor, not something it makes unnecessary."],null],["cp36",0,3,"According to cloud breach data, which is the MOST common cause of cloud data exposure?",["Customer misconfiguration of cloud resources","VM escape through hypervisor flaws","Side-channel attacks between tenants","Physical theft of provider hardware"],0,"Misconfigurations such as public storage and over-broad permissions cause far more incidents than sophisticated infrastructure attacks.","Domain 3",[null,"VM escape is severe but rare in practice.","Side-channel attacks are real but seldom the cause of actual data exposures.","Provider data centers have strong physical controls, and physical theft is a very rare cause."],null],["cp37",0,3,"Researchers have launched hundreds of cloud instances, many with no owner and months of missing patches. Which risk does this describe?",["VM escape","VM sprawl","Hypervisor hyperjacking","Resource exhaustion by a neighbor"],1,"VM sprawl is uncontrolled growth of unmanaged virtual machines, which leaves unpatched, unowned systems exposed.","Domain 3",["VM escape is breaking out of a VM to the hypervisor, which the scenario does not describe.",null,"Hyperjacking is taking control of the hypervisor itself, not an accumulation of unmanaged guests.","A noisy neighbor consumes shared resources; the scenario is about unmanaged instances."],null],["cp38",0,3,"How does a public cloud customer normally gain assurance about the provider's data center physical security?",["By performing its own on-site inspection of each data center","By installing its own cameras in the provider's facility","By reviewing independent audit reports and certifications","By requiring the provider to share the badge access logs"],2,"Large providers do not allow individual customer visits; independent reports such as SOC 2 Type II and ISO/IEC 27001 cover physical controls.","Domain 3",["Public providers generally do not permit individual customer inspections of multitenant data centers.","Customers cannot place their own equipment for monitoring in a provider's shared facility.",null,"Raw access logs of shared facilities include other parties' data and are not provided to customers."],null],["cp39",0,3,"Eight engineers log in daily with the cloud account's root credentials. What should be done FIRST?",["Move daily work to federated admin roles and lock away the root credentials","Change the root password every month and share it with the engineers through a password vault","Enable detailed billing reports for the account","Add a second root account for redundancy"],0,"Root credentials should not be used for routine work; individual federated roles with MFA give least privilege and accountability, while root is secured and monitored.","Domain 3",[null,"Rotating a shared root password still leaves everyone using an all-powerful shared identity.","Billing reports do not address the misuse of privileged credentials.","Adding more all-powerful accounts increases risk instead of reducing it."],null],["cp40",0,3,"Return traffic to a web server is being blocked even though inbound HTTPS is allowed at the subnet level. What is the MOST likely cause?",["A stateful security group is dropping replies","The TLS certificate on the server has expired","The route table has no internet gateway route for inbound traffic","A stateless network ACL lacks an outbound rule"],3,"Network ACLs are usually stateless, so both directions must be allowed. Security groups are stateful and permit return traffic automatically.","Domain 3",["Stateful security groups automatically allow responses to permitted inbound traffic.","An expired certificate causes TLS errors, not network-level blocking of return traffic.","Inbound traffic is already reaching the server, so routing for inbound is not the problem.",null],null],["cp41",0,3,"After a breach, an attacker moved from a compromised web server to database servers in the same subnet. Which control would BEST limit this lateral movement?",["Microsegmentation between workloads","A larger subnet address range","A web application firewall in front of the site","Longer retention of flow logs"],0,"Microsegmentation restricts communication between individual workloads to only required paths, even within one subnet.","Domain 3",[null,"Subnet size does not restrict traffic between hosts within it.","A WAF filters inbound web requests but does not control server-to-server traffic.","Flow logs help investigation but do not prevent movement."],null],["cp42",0,3,"Which principle is central to a zero trust architecture?",["Trust all traffic that originates inside the virtual network","Verify every request based on identity and context","Rely on the VPN as the single security boundary","Allow administrators unrestricted standing access"],1,"Zero trust removes implicit trust based on network location and verifies identity, device and context for every access, with least privilege.","Domain 3",["Implicit trust for internal traffic is exactly what zero trust rejects.",null,"A single perimeter boundary such as a VPN is the model zero trust replaces.","Standing unrestricted access violates least privilege, a core zero trust tenet."],null],["cp43",0,3,"A workload has an RTO of 4 hours and an RPO of 1 hour, and budget is tight. Which DR strategy is MOST appropriate?",["Active-active deployment across two regions running at full capacity","Weekly backups restored manually after a disaster","No DR plan, relying on the provider's SLA credits","Pilot light in a second region with hourly replication"],3,"Pilot light keeps core data replicated and minimal systems ready to scale, meeting a 4-hour RTO and 1-hour RPO at modest cost.","Domain 3",["Active-active meets the targets but at far higher cost than needed.","Weekly backups cannot meet a one-hour RPO and manual restore may exceed four hours.","SLA credits compensate financially but do not recover the service.",null],null],["cp44",0,3,"Which DR test actually fails over production systems to the recovery site and carries the MOST risk?",["Tabletop exercise","Parallel test","Full interruption test","Checklist review"],2,"A full interruption test shuts down or fails over production, proving recovery but risking real outage.","Domain 3",["A tabletop exercise is a discussion and does not touch systems.","A parallel test recovers systems at the alternate site without stopping production.",null,"A checklist review only verifies that plan documents are complete."],null],["cp45",0,3,"An IaaS customer needs packet-level visibility of traffic to its own instances for an investigation. Which option is available?",["The provider's traffic mirroring feature for the instances' interfaces","A physical tap on the provider's top-of-rack switch","Promiscuous mode to capture neighbors' traffic","Requesting full packet captures of the provider's backbone network for the incident window"],0,"Customers cannot touch physical networks; provider traffic mirroring or host-based capture on their own instances gives packet visibility.","Domain 3",[null,"Customers have no access to the provider's physical switches.","Tenant isolation prevents seeing other tenants' traffic, and trying to would be inappropriate.","Providers do not hand out backbone captures, which contain other customers' data."],null],["cp46",0,3,"A SIEM alert links a sign-in from a new country, creation of a new access key and a large storage download within 15 minutes. Which audit mechanism produced this insight?",["Packet capture","Correlation","Log retention","Flow sampling"],1,"Correlation links related events from several sources to reveal a pattern that no single event shows.","Domain 3",["Packet capture records traffic contents; it does not link identity and storage events.",null,"Retention keeps logs available but does not analyze relationships between them.","Flow sampling reduces network data volume; it does not tie together events from different services."],null],["cp47",0,3,"Which item is a physical and environmental protection control in a data center?",["Security groups on virtual machines","Encryption of data in transit with TLS","A mantrap at the entrance to the server hall","Federated single sign-on for administrators"],2,"A mantrap physically controls entry so only one authenticated person passes at a time.","Domain 3",["Security groups are logical network controls, not physical ones.","TLS is a system and communications protection control.",null,"Single sign-on is an identification and authentication control."],null],["cp48",0,3,"In software-defined networking, what does the control plane do?",["Physically carries packets between hosts","Encrypts all tenant traffic","Stores the tenants' virtual machine images","Decides how traffic should be forwarded"],3,"SDN separates the control plane, which makes forwarding decisions and can be programmed via APIs, from the data plane, which forwards packets.","Domain 3",["Carrying packets is the job of the data plane.","Encryption is a separate function and not the definition of the control plane.","Image storage is a storage service, unrelated to network control.",null],null],["cp49",0,4,"During design review, a team finds that a user could upload files that appear to come from another user. Which STRIDE category is this?",["Tampering","Repudiation","Spoofing","Elevation of privilege"],2,"Spoofing is pretending to be someone else; it violates authentication.","Domain 4",["Tampering is unauthorized modification of data, not impersonation.","Repudiation is denying having performed an action, which relates to missing audit evidence.",null,"Elevation of privilege is gaining rights beyond those granted."],null],["cp50",0,4,"Which threat modeling method is a seven-stage, risk-centric process that starts from business objectives?",["STRIDE","DREAD","ATASM","PASTA"],3,"PASTA (Process for Attack Simulation and Threat Analysis) has seven stages beginning with business objectives and ending with risk and impact analysis.","Domain 4",["STRIDE is a classification of threat types, not a seven-stage process.","DREAD is a scoring model for rating threats.","ATASM is a four-step sequence: architecture, threats, attack surfaces, mitigations.",null],null],["cp51",0,4,"An image-preview feature fetches any URL a user supplies. A tester uses it to read temporary credentials from the instance metadata service. What vulnerability is this?",["Cross-site scripting (XSS)","Server-side request forgery (SSRF)","Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)","SQL injection"],1,"SSRF makes a server send requests to attacker-chosen destinations, such as the metadata endpoint, which is a classic cloud credential theft path.","Domain 4",["XSS injects script into pages viewed by other users; it does not make the server fetch internal URLs.",null,"CSRF abuses a user's browser to send unwanted requests to a trusted site.","SQL injection targets database queries, not outbound server requests."],null],["cp52",0,4,"Which testing approach analyzes source code for security flaws without executing the application?",["Static application security testing (SAST)","Dynamic application security testing (DAST)","Interactive application security testing (IAST)","Load and performance testing"],0,"SAST examines code at rest early in development and points to specific lines.","Domain 4",[null,"DAST tests a running application from outside.","IAST uses an agent inside a running application during tests.","Load testing measures performance under demand, not code flaws."],null],["cp53",0,4,"A critical vulnerability is announced in a popular open-source logging library. Which tool helps a company find which of its services include it?",["A web application firewall","Dynamic application security testing","A network vulnerability scanner","Software composition analysis with SBOMs"],3,"SCA inventories third-party components and, with software bills of materials, shows exactly where a library is used.","Domain 4",["A WAF may block some exploit attempts but does not inventory components.","DAST tests running apps from outside and may miss a library that is not reachable.","Network scanners look at hosts and ports, not libraries inside application builds.",null],null],["cp54",0,4,"Which OWASP ASVS level is recommended for most applications that handle sensitive data?",["Level 2","Level 1","Level 3","Level 0"],0,"Level 2 is the recommended standard level for most applications with sensitive data; Level 3 is for the most critical systems.","Domain 4",[null,"Level 1 is a basic minimum suitable for all applications, not the recommended level for sensitive data.","Level 3 is reserved for the most critical, high-assurance applications.","The ASVS defines levels 1 to 3; there is no Level 0 verification level."],null],["cp55",0,4,"What is the MOST effective defense against SQL injection in a cloud-hosted application?",["A web application firewall only","Parameterized queries","Encrypting the database at rest","Hiding detailed error messages"],1,"Parameterized queries keep user input separate from SQL code, removing the root cause. A WAF is a compensating control.","Domain 4",["A WAF can block known patterns but can be bypassed and does not fix the code.",null,"Encryption at rest does not stop the application from executing injected queries.","Hiding errors makes exploitation harder to see but leaves the flaw in place."],null],["cp56",0,4,"A malicious public package with the same name as a company's internal library gets pulled into a build. Which control would BEST prevent this?",["Running DAST against the staging environment","Adding a WAF in front of the build server","Pulling packages only from a curated internal repository","Increasing peer code review of the team's own application logic"],2,"This is dependency confusion; resolving packages only through a curated internal repository or proxy, with pinned versions, prevents public look-alikes being fetched.","Domain 4",["DAST tests a running app and would not stop a malicious dependency being built in.","A WAF protects web apps from inbound requests, not package resolution during builds.",null,"Reviewing the team's own logic does not inspect how dependencies are resolved."],null],["cp57",0,4,"Partners call a company's REST APIs. The company needs per-partner quotas, token validation and central logging. Which component fits BEST?",["Database activity monitor","XML firewall","API gateway","Application sandbox"],2,"An API gateway authenticates callers, enforces quotas and rate limits, routes requests and logs traffic in one place.","Domain 4",["DAM monitors database queries, not API authentication and quotas.","An XML firewall validates XML messages; it is not the general front door for REST APIs with quotas.",null,"A sandbox isolates untrusted code; it does not manage API access."],null],["cp58",0,4,"A service account that normally reads a few rows starts reading an entire customer table at 3 a.m. Which control is designed to detect this?",["Web application firewall","Database activity monitoring","TLS certificate pinning","Static code analysis"],1,"DAM watches database queries and alerts on unusual activity, including misuse by authorized accounts.","Domain 4",["A WAF inspects HTTP traffic and would not see direct database queries.",null,"Certificate pinning protects connections from interception, not misuse of valid access.","Static analysis reviews code, not runtime query behavior."],null],["cp59",0,4,"Which statement about OAuth 2.0 is correct?",["It is an authorization framework for delegated API access","It is an XML standard for enterprise single sign-on","It authenticates users by itself without any identity layer","It replaces the need for TLS between client and server"],0,"OAuth 2.0 lets an app obtain limited access to an API on a user's behalf. OpenID Connect adds authentication on top; SAML is the XML SSO standard.","Domain 4",[null,"That describes SAML 2.0, not OAuth.","OAuth alone does not define user authentication; OpenID Connect adds that layer.","OAuth relies on TLS to protect tokens in transit."],null],["cp60",0,4,"A security team wants to discover which unsanctioned cloud applications employees use and apply data policies to them. Which tool is designed for this?",["Hardware security module (HSM)","Database activity monitor","Identity provider","Cloud access security broker (CASB)"],3,"A CASB gives visibility into cloud app use, including shadow IT, and enforces access, DLP and threat policies.","Domain 4",["An HSM protects cryptographic keys; it does not discover cloud app usage.","A DAM monitors database queries, not SaaS usage by employees.","An IdP authenticates users to federated apps but does not discover unsanctioned apps.",null],null],["cp61",0,4,"In which SDLC phase does threat modeling provide the MOST value?",["Deployment","Design","Operations","Disposal"],1,"Threat modeling in design finds architectural flaws when they are cheapest to fix, before code exists.","Domain 4",["By deployment, design flaws are built in and expensive to change.",null,"In operations, fixing design flaws may require redesign and emergency releases.","At disposal, the system is being retired and threat modeling offers little benefit."],null],["cp62",0,4,"Before running a penetration test against its own workloads hosted on a public cloud, what should the customer do FIRST?",["Test the provider's hypervisor for escape flaws","Scan other tenants' IP ranges for comparison","Disable logging to avoid alert noise","Review the provider's penetration testing policy"],3,"Providers set rules on what customers may test; following them avoids violating terms or disrupting shared infrastructure.","Domain 4",["Attacking the provider's underlying platform is almost always prohibited.","Scanning other tenants is unauthorized and potentially illegal.","Disabling logging removes evidence and monitoring; tests should be coordinated, not hidden.",null],null],["cp63",0,5,"Which hardware component allows a host to prove that it booted with approved firmware and software?",["Trusted platform module (TPM)","Baseboard management controller","Network interface card","Hardware load balancer"],0,"A TPM records boot measurements, supporting secure and measured boot and remote attestation.","Domain 5",[null,"A BMC provides out-of-band management; it is a target to protect, not a boot integrity anchor.","A network card moves traffic and has no role in measuring the boot process.","A load balancer distributes traffic and does not attest to host boot integrity."],null],["cp64",0,5,"A platform team wants every new VM to start from a patched, hardened and scanned baseline. What should it implement?",["Manual hardening checklists for each new VM","Snapshots of production VMs copied to new projects","A catalog of signed golden images","Default marketplace images used as provided"],2,"Golden images built to a benchmark, scanned, signed and published to an approved catalog give consistent secure starting points.","Domain 5",["Manual checklists are slow and inconsistent compared with pre-built images.","Copying production snapshots spreads data and secrets and does not guarantee hardening.",null,"Unmodified marketplace images may not meet the organization's hardening baseline."],null],["cp65",0,5,"What does DNSSEC provide?",["Confidentiality of DNS queries between clients and resolvers","Load balancing of DNS servers","Automatic IP address assignment","Integrity and authenticity of DNS responses"],3,"DNSSEC signs DNS records so resolvers can verify they are authentic and unaltered, defending against cache poisoning. It does not encrypt queries.","Domain 5",["DNSSEC does not encrypt queries; encrypted DNS transports address confidentiality.","Load balancing is unrelated to DNSSEC's signing function.","Automatic address assignment is DHCP's job.",null],null],["cp66",0,5,"An operations team wants administrators to reach private cloud servers without any inbound SSH port open to the internet. Which approach BEST achieves this?",["A provider-managed session service with SSO and MFA","Opening SSH to the internet with a strong password","Changing SSH to a non-standard port","Sharing one SSH key among all administrators"],0,"Managed session services broker access through the management plane without open inbound ports, with centralized authentication and session logging.","Domain 5",[null,"Internet-exposed SSH remains an attack target regardless of password strength.","A non-standard port is obscurity; scanners still find it.","A shared key destroys individual accountability and is hard to revoke."],null],["cp67",0,5,"An engineer manually opened a firewall rule in the console, so the live environment no longer matches its Terraform code. What is this called?",["Immutable infrastructure","Configuration drift","Blue-green deployment","Policy as code"],1,"Drift is divergence between the actual configuration and the approved baseline or code, often from manual changes that bypass review.","Domain 5",["Immutable infrastructure is the practice that prevents in-place changes like this.",null,"Blue-green deployment is a release strategy that switches between two environments.","Policy as code enforces rules automatically; it is a control against drift, not the drift itself."],null],["cp68",0,5,"In a managed PaaS database service, who is responsible for patching the database engine?",["The customer's database administrator","The customer's application developers","The cloud provider","An independent third-party auditor"],2,"In PaaS the provider maintains the platform, including the database engine; the customer manages configuration, access and data.","Domain 5",["Customers do not have access to patch the engine in a managed service.","Developers manage application code, not the managed platform underneath.",null,"Auditors assess controls; they do not operate or patch systems."],null],["cp69",0,5,"A critical patch cannot be applied to a legacy application server for three months. What is the BEST course of action?",["Leave the server unchanged until the vendor eventually offers a supported replacement","Apply the patch directly in production without testing","Document an exception with compensating controls and a remediation date","Remove the server from the asset inventory"],2,"An approved, time-limited exception with compensating controls such as isolation and extra monitoring manages the risk until remediation.","Domain 5",["Doing nothing leaves an undocumented, unmanaged risk.","Untested production patching risks outages; the scenario says the patch cannot be applied yet.",null,"Hiding the asset from inventory removes visibility and makes the risk worse."],null],["cp70",0,5,"What BEST proves that cloud backups will support recovery within the RTO?",["Regular test restores measured against the RTO","Increasing the backup frequency to every hour","Storing backups in a second region","Encrypting backups with customer-managed keys"],0,"Only restoring and timing it proves recovery works and meets the objective.","Domain 5",[null,"More frequent backups improve RPO but prove nothing about restore time.","A second region protects against regional loss but does not show restores work.","Encryption protects confidentiality, not recoverability."],null],["cp71",0,5,"An application fails to scale during a traffic spike because the account reached its limit on running instances. What should capacity monitoring have included?",["Hypervisor firmware versions","Service quotas and account limits","TLS cipher suite settings","Data classification labels"],1,"In the cloud, quotas can stop scaling as surely as hardware shortages, so they belong in capacity monitoring and planning.","Domain 5",["Customers do not manage hypervisor firmware, and it does not limit instance counts.",null,"Cipher suites affect security of connections, not the number of instances allowed.","Classification labels guide data handling, not capacity."],null],["cp72",0,5,"Three outages in a month were caused by expired certificates. Service was restored quickly each time. Which ITIL process should now find and remove the root cause?",["Incident management","Release management","Service level management","Problem management"],3,"Problem management identifies and eliminates root causes of recurring incidents; incident management restores service.","Domain 5",["Incident management focuses on restoring service fast, which already happened each time.","Release management moves changes into production; it does not investigate root causes.","Service level management agrees and tracks targets, not root-cause analysis.",null],null],["cp73",0,5,"What is the primary purpose of a configuration management database (CMDB) during change management?",["Storing application secrets and passwords used during automated deployment","Recording customer support tickets","Holding encrypted backups of servers","Showing configuration items and dependencies to assess impact"],3,"A CMDB records configuration items and their relationships, which lets teams judge what a change will affect.","Domain 5",["Secrets belong in a secrets manager, not a CMDB.","Support tickets live in a service desk tool, not in the CMDB.","Backups are stored in backup systems, not the CMDB.",null],null],["cp74",0,5,"A web server in IaaS shows signs of compromise. What should the responder do FIRST to preserve evidence while stopping the attack?",["Isolate it with a restrictive security group, then capture memory and snapshot disks","Terminate the instance immediately and launch a clean replacement from the golden image","Reboot the instance to clear the attacker's processes","Restore the instance from last night's backup"],0,"Isolation contains the threat without destroying volatile evidence; memory and disk captures preserve it for analysis.","Domain 5",[null,"Terminating destroys memory and any ephemeral storage before they are captured.","Rebooting wipes volatile evidence such as running processes and memory.","Restoring overwrites the compromised state before it has been preserved."],null],["cp75",0,5,"Which document records who collected each piece of digital evidence and everyone who handled it afterwards?",["Rules of engagement","Chain of custody","Business impact analysis","Service level agreement"],1,"The chain of custody is the unbroken record of collection, handling, transfer and storage that makes evidence trustworthy.","Domain 5",["Rules of engagement define the scope and limits of a security test.",null,"A BIA identifies critical processes and recovery objectives.","An SLA defines service commitments between provider and customer."],null],["cp76",0,5,"During an incident affecting customer data, a technical engineer is contacted by a journalist. What should the engineer do?",["Explain the technical details of the attack to correct any rumors quickly","Confirm the number of affected customers","Refer the journalist to the designated communications function","Deny that any incident has occurred"],2,"Communication follows the communication plan and authority; public statements come from designated spokespeople coordinated with legal.","Domain 5",["Unauthorized technical disclosure can breach legal and contractual duties and harm the investigation.","Confirming figures is for authorized spokespeople after legal review.",null,"Denying a real incident is dishonest and can create legal liability."],null],["cp77",0,5,"A SOAR playbook detects an access key used from an anonymizing network to list storage buckets. What should the automated containment step be?",["Delete all storage buckets in the account","Disable the compromised access key","Shut down the SIEM to reduce alert noise","Rotate the TLS certificates on the web servers"],1,"Revoking or disabling compromised credentials is the typical first containment action in the cloud.","Domain 5",["Deleting buckets destroys data and evidence and is not proportionate.",null,"Disabling the SIEM removes visibility during an active incident.","TLS certificates are unrelated to a stolen access key."],null],["cp78",0,5,"What is a key limitation of ML-based anomaly detection in security operations?",["It can only detect known malware signatures","It cannot process cloud audit logs","It never produces false positives","Slow attackers can blend into the learned baseline"],3,"Anomaly models learn normal behavior; patient attackers acting within normal patterns, or activity present during training, can go undetected.","Domain 5",["Signature matching is a different technique; anomaly detection aims at unknown behavior.","Cloud audit logs are a common input to anomaly detection services.","Anomaly detection commonly produces false positives when legitimate behavior changes.",null],null],["cp79",0,6,"A European company stores data only in EU regions of a provider headquartered in another country. Why might foreign law still reach that data?",["The provider may be compelled by its home country's laws","EU regions are legally part of the provider's home country","Data in the cloud is not subject to any national law","Encryption at rest automatically transfers jurisdiction"],0,"A provider can be subject to its home jurisdiction's legal orders for data it controls anywhere, creating conflicts with local privacy law.","Domain 6",[null,"Data centers in the EU are under EU member state jurisdiction, not the provider's home country.","Cloud data is subject to laws in several jurisdictions, not none.","Encryption does not change jurisdiction, though customer-held keys can limit what is disclosed."],null],["cp80",0,6,"Which international standard series provides guidance on electronic discovery?",["ISO/IEC 27037","ISO/IEC 27036","ISO/IEC 27050","ISO/IEC 31000"],2,"ISO/IEC 27050 covers eDiscovery: identification, preservation, collection, processing, review and production of electronically stored information.","Domain 6",["ISO/IEC 27037 covers identification, collection, acquisition and preservation of digital evidence.","ISO/IEC 27036 covers information security in supplier relationships.",null,"ISO/IEC 31000 gives general risk management guidelines."],null],["cp81",0,6,"A company uses a SaaS CRM to store its customers' personal data. Under the GDPR, what role does the SaaS provider usually play?",["Controller","Data subject","Supervisory authority","Processor"],3,"The company decides the purposes and means (controller); the provider processes data on its instructions (processor).","Domain 6",["The controller is the company that determines why and how data is processed.","Data subjects are the individuals the data describes.","The supervisory authority is the regulator that enforces the GDPR.",null],null],["cp82",0,6,"A US clinic plans to store patient records with a cloud provider. Which agreement does HIPAA require with that provider?",["Business associate agreement (BAA)","Master services agreement only","Standard contractual clauses","Non-disclosure agreement only"],0,"HIPAA requires a BAA with service providers that create, receive, maintain or transmit protected health information for a covered entity.","Domain 6",[null,"An MSA sets general terms but does not by itself contain the HIPAA-required obligations.","Standard contractual clauses are a GDPR mechanism for international transfers.","An NDA covers confidentiality but not the specific safeguards HIPAA requires."],null],["cp83",0,6,"When should a privacy impact assessment be performed for a new cloud-based HR system?",["After the first data breach","Before the system is implemented","Only during the annual external audit","When the system is decommissioned"],1,"A PIA identifies privacy risks early so they can be mitigated in design and contracts.","Domain 6",["Waiting for a breach defeats the preventive purpose of the assessment.",null,"Annual audits may review PIAs but are not when they should first be done.","At decommissioning it is too late to shape how data is collected and used."],null],["cp84",0,6,"A customer reviewing a provider's SOC 2 report sees a section listing controls the customer must operate itself. What is this section?",["Management's assertion","Subservice organization carve-out","Complementary user entity controls","Auditor's opinion letter"],2,"Complementary user entity controls are the customer-side controls needed for the provider's described controls to be effective.","Domain 6",["Management's assertion is the provider's statement about its system, not a list of customer duties.","A carve-out excludes a subservice organization's controls from the report's scope.",null,"The opinion letter gives the auditor's conclusion, not customer responsibilities."],null],["cp85",0,6,"A company buys cyber insurance to cover losses from a cloud provider outage. Which risk treatment is this, and what does it NOT transfer?",["Risk avoidance; the outage risk is eliminated","Risk acceptance; no action is taken","Risk transfer; accountability for the data and reputation remain","Risk mitigation; the likelihood of an outage at the provider is reduced"],2,"Insurance transfers financial impact, but accountability, regulatory duties and reputational harm stay with the organization.","Domain 6",["Avoidance means not doing the activity; insurance does not remove the outage risk.","Buying insurance is an action, so it is not simple acceptance.",null,"Insurance does not reduce the chance of an outage, so it is not mitigation."],null],["cp86",0,6,"Which NIST publication defines the Risk Management Framework that underpins FedRAMP authorizations?",["NIST SP 800-37","NIST SP 800-145","NIST SP 800-88","NIST SP 800-61"],0,"SP 800-37 defines the RMF steps: prepare, categorize, select, implement, assess, authorize and monitor.","Domain 6",[null,"SP 800-145 is the NIST definition of cloud computing.","SP 800-88 covers media sanitization.","SP 800-61 is the computer security incident handling guide."],null],["cp87",0,6,"A company is worried that leaving its SaaS provider in the future will be costly and could lose data. Which contract terms BEST address this?",["Higher service credits for missed availability","A longer initial contract term","A larger limitation of liability for the provider","Exit terms covering data export format, timing and deletion"],3,"Exit and portability terms guarantee data can be retrieved in a usable format and then deleted, reducing lock-in.","Domain 6",["Service credits compensate for downtime but do not help retrieve data on exit.","A longer term deepens commitment and does nothing for portability.","Liability limits concern damages, not the ability to leave with your data.",null],null],["cp88",0,6,"Which standard series addresses information security in supplier relationships, including a part specific to cloud services?",["ISO/IEC 27017","ISO/IEC 27036","ISO/IEC 20000-1","ISO/IEC 42001"],1,"ISO/IEC 27036 covers supplier relationship security across the lifecycle and includes guidance for cloud services.","Domain 6",["ISO/IEC 27017 gives cloud security controls but is not a supplier relationship standard.",null,"ISO/IEC 20000-1 specifies IT service management system requirements.","ISO/IEC 42001 specifies AI management system requirements."],null],["cp89",0,6,"A bank plans to use a cloud AI model to pre-screen loan applications. Which measure BEST addresses its ethical and regulatory obligations?",["Relying on the provider's model card as proof of fairness","Bias testing with human review of rejections","Hiding AI use from applicants to avoid complaints","Letting the model make final decisions to ensure consistency"],1,"High-risk AI uses call for bias testing, human oversight, transparency and accountability, which remain the bank's responsibility.","Domain 6",["A provider's documentation helps but does not prove fairness for the bank's own data and use.",null,"Transparency obligations generally require telling people when AI affects decisions about them.","Removing human oversight from significant decisions conflicts with accountability expectations."],null],["cp90",0,6,"A retailer assumes its checkout is PCI DSS compliant because its cloud provider holds a PCI DSS attestation. What must the retailer verify?",["That the provider's attestation is dated within the last five years and is signed","That the provider also holds a SOC 3 report","That its services are in the provider's scope and its own controls comply","That its payment pages use a content delivery network"],2,"A provider's attestation covers only listed services and the provider's responsibilities; the customer's configuration and processes need their own compliance evidence.","Domain 6",["PCI DSS validation is annual, and age alone says nothing about scope or customer controls.","A SOC 3 report is a general summary and does not establish PCI DSS compliance.",null,"A CDN is a performance choice and does not demonstrate compliance."],null]]);
